+**Winning is not the same as arriving all at once.** A forward step large
+enough to matter — an operator setting the date, a VM resuming after being
+suspended for hours — would otherwise land on `ucal wallclock`'s face as a
+single frame skipping straight to the correction. One reading may advance past
+the last one by at most **twice the real (monotonic) time elapsed since the
+previous reading**: a backward step is untouched by this, since the monotonic
+branch already advances at exactly the oscillator's rate, but a large forward
+step is spread over the following readings instead — the face runs fast for a
+moment rather than jumping — until it lands on the correction and tracking
+resumes at the ordinary rate. Nothing is refused; only the rate at which this
+process's own readings may move towards the correction is bounded.
